---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Six queues and a reserved worker — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="How LightPhotos loads images in the background, told as the problems that caused each part of the design. None of the problems are specific to photos."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-08-20">August 20, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Six queues and a reserved worker</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          This post tells how LightPhotos loads images in the background. I
          tell it as a series of problems. Six queues and a worker that refuses
          the most common job can look like too much. But I added each part
          because something broke visibly without it.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 5 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          In LightPhotos, a photo that you opened could wait behind a hundred
          thumbnails that you scrolled past before. I fixed this problem in
          layers. I made separate queues by urgency, and I kept one worker free
          for the photo that you open. I also fixed the bugs that these changes
          caused. If you make software with background workers, you will
          probably get most of these problems too, and none of them are about
          photos.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Urgent work at the front of the queue is not
          sufficient.</strong> If all workers are busy with background jobs,
          the urgent job still waits. Keeping one worker free is what
          actually got it through.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Try to keep a worker free before you try to cancel
          work.</strong> Keeping one worker free took a few lines. You usually
          cannot stop a load halfway through inside someone else's library.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A worker that can refuse jobs changes the rules.</strong> The
          usual way to wake workers assumes that any worker can take any job.
          When that stopped being true, work just sat there with no error.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Every "someone is handling this" flag needs someone to
          clear it.</strong> Think through every way that someone might not
          exist. Then make the code that queues the job say which case happened.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>One queue, several workers</h2>
      <p>
        LightPhotos loads (decodes) images on a few background threads. I call
        these threads workers. The workers keep the app responsive while they
        decode. My first version was the simplest thing I could think of. It
        had one queue and a handful of workers, and they took jobs in the
        order they arrived.
      </p>
      <p>
        It worked until I scrolled. When you scroll the grid, the app
        queues a hundred thumbnails. Then you double-click a photo, and the
        photo goes to the end of the queue. It waits behind a hundred
        thumbnails that you do not look at now. The app looks frozen while it
        completes work for a part of the screen that you scrolled past. This
        is frustrating when you only want to see your photo.
      </p>

      <h2>Some jobs are more important</h2>
      <p>
        So I split the one queue into six. I ordered them by how much you
        care about the result right now. The photo that you opened goes in
        front of the thumbnails.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 240" role="img" aria-label="Diagram: six priority queues send jobs in order to a pool of eight decode workers. One worker is reserved and never accepts thumbnail jobs.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="24">WORKERS TAKE JOBS IN THIS ORDER</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="0" y="36" width="300" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="12" y="53">1</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="30" y="53">First pass of the photo you opened</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="0" y="68" width="300" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="12" y="85">2</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="30" y="85">Sharp second pass of that photo</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="100" width="300" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="12" y="117">3</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="30" y="117">Full-resolution decode, on demand</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="132" width="300" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="12" y="149">4</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="30" y="149">Camera and lens metadata</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="164" width="300" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="12" y="181">5</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="30" y="181">Thumbnails for the grid</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="196" width="300" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="12" y="213">6</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="30" y="213">Capture times for burst groups</text>

          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="310" y1="96" x2="368" y2="96"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M368 90 L368 102 L380 96 Z"></path>

          <path class="lp-fig-line-soft" d="M300 177 H340 V221 H374" stroke-dasharray="3 4"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" d="M332 191 L348 207 M348 191 L332 207"></path>

          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="380" y="24">8 DECODE WORKERS</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="380" y="36" width="240" height="20" rx="4"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="380" y="61" width="240" height="20" rx="4"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="380" y="86" width="240" height="20" rx="4"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="380" y="111" width="240" height="20" rx="4"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="380" y="136" width="240" height="20" rx="4"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="380" y="161" width="240" height="20" rx="4"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="380" y="186" width="240" height="20" rx="4"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="380" y="211" width="240" height="20" rx="4"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="392" y="226">reserved &middot; never takes thumbnails</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          Camera details (metadata) come before thumbnails, and this order can
          be a surprise. The details panel is next to the photo that you look
          at now, so you would see a delay there. A thumbnail two rows down is
          not on the screen yet.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        I ordered the queues by what you are looking at right now. How quick a
        job is did not matter.
      </p>

      <h2>The first position in the queue is not sufficient</h2>
      <p>
        With six queues, the freeze was shorter, but it did not stop. Priority
        only decides which job in the queue <em>starts</em> next. It has no
        effect on jobs that already run. Suppose all eight workers picked up
        thumbnails one millisecond before you double-clicked. Then your photo
        is at the front of the queue, but no worker is free to take it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The usual solution is to interrupt a job that runs and give its worker
        to the urgent job. I could not do this. The loading happens inside
        someone else's library, and it has no way to know that I
        want it to stop.
      </p>
      <p>
        What I could do was keep one worker aside. This worker never takes
        thumbnails. It sits idle while the other workers decode thumbnails,
        and it is ready immediately when you open a photo. On a computer with
        only one CPU core, I turn this off. If I set aside the only
        worker, the grid has nothing left.
      </p>

      <h2>A worker that can refuse jobs broke the wake-up signal</h2>
      <p>
        Workers sleep when the queue is empty. When the app adds a job, it
        wakes one worker. This is the standard method, and it works if each
        sleeping worker can take each job. My reserved worker cannot take each
        job. It can refuse a job.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 190" role="img" aria-label="Sequence: the app queues a thumbnail and wakes one worker. This worker is the reserved worker. It refuses the job and sleeps again. The app never wakes the other seven workers.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">WAKE ONE WORKER, WHEN ONE WORKER CAN REFUSE</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="32" width="140" height="48" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="70" y="52" text-anchor="middle">Thumbnail</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="70" y="68" text-anchor="middle">queued</text>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M146 50 L146 62 L158 56 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="170" y="32" width="140" height="48" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="240" y="52" text-anchor="middle">Wake one</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="240" y="68" text-anchor="middle">worker</text>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M316 50 L316 62 L328 56 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="340" y="32" width="220" height="48" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="450" y="52" text-anchor="middle">The reserved worker looks,</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="450" y="68" text-anchor="middle">refuses, and sleeps again</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="340" y="104" width="22" height="22" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="371" y="104" width="22" height="22" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="402" y="104" width="22" height="22" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="433" y="104" width="22" height="22" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="464" y="104" width="22" height="22" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="495" y="104" width="22" height="22" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="526" y="104" width="22" height="22" rx="3"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="340" y="148">nothing wakes the other seven</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s lp-fig-bad" x="0" y="112">No error. No crash.</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="132">The work stays in the queue until</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="152">an unrelated event wakes a worker.</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          The fix is to wake all workers instead of only one.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        To wake only one worker is correct only if each worker can do each
        job. When I let one worker refuse jobs, this condition was not true.
        The app did not crash or show an error. The work stayed in the queue
        until an unrelated event woke a worker.
      </p>

      <h2>Markers for work that no worker started</h2>
      <p>
        The loader keeps a list of "already requested" markers. With these
        markers, it does not queue the same photo five times. It clears each
        marker when the job is complete. In the browser version, the worker
        pool does not start threads. So nothing was ever going to finish
        those jobs or clear the markers.
      </p>
      <p>
        No error message occurred. Instead, the app woke sixty times each
        second for the rest of the session. It did this to check on work
        that did not exist, and it drained your laptop battery.
      </p>
      <p>
        I fixed this problem with a new rule: each marker must have an owner
        that clears it. When the app queues a job, it gets one of three
        answers: accepted, no workers exist, or the worker pool is broken. The
        code marks the job as in progress only for the first answer. I now use
        this rule for each "loading" flag or "already requested" list. Set the
        flag only after the owner that will clear it accepts the job.
      </p>

      <h2>In a browser, workers can fail</h2>
      <p>
        When I moved the worker pool into a browser tab, I had to watch the
        workers much more closely. A new browser worker (a Web Worker)
        discards each message that it gets before its script is ready. So
        each worker now says when it is ready. If one stays quiet for ten
        seconds, I treat it as failed.
      </p>
      <p>
        I replace a failed worker up to three times. Then I mark that
        slot as permanently gone. I kept this state separate from "not ready
        yet" on purpose, because mixing them up caused a real hang.
      </p>
      <p>
        Exporting waits while the number of running jobs is at or above
        capacity. Capacity counts only the workers that are ready.
        Without a "permanently gone" state, a slot stuck halfway through
        replacement looks not ready. Then the capacity decreases to zero, and
        the export progress bar waits forever.
      </p>

      <h2>What made this design easy to test</h2>
      <p>
        I can test all of this without real threads. The rules that decide
        which worker runs which job are in a plain data structure. One test
        builds this structure and adds one job of each type in reverse priority
        order. Then it takes them all back out and checks the order. Another
        test takes jobs out as the reserved worker and checks that it skips
        thumbnails.
      </p>
      <p>
        Parallel code usually has a predictable core. This core is the set of
        rules about which worker runs which job, and in which order. Around
        this core is a layer with timing that changes from run to run. When I
        pulled the rules out on their own, I could test my decisions in
        microseconds. The tests give the same result every time.
      </p>

